icecatrc: IceCat, set up for privacy
====================================

Written with an AI (vibecoded), for one person's computer; read what
it does before you use it. Improvements are welcome.

IceCat's settings, three add-ons, and our extension "switch". The
dotfiles install it all as the part "icecatrc" (../install.sh icecatrc,
and in "all"); icecatrc/install.sh does the work and can be run on its
own. These notes say what it is for and how it is built. Read them
before changing anything.


1. The goal
-----------

A privacy WebExtension for GNU IceCat, called "switch", plus scripts
that configure an IceCat that is already installed. It must:

- look dark, in IceCat's style, with habamax colours (#1c1c1c ground,
  #bcbcbc text, #5f87af links, #af87af visited);
- be usable from the keyboard alone: every action has a key;
- switch JavaScript off and on, globally and per site;
- switch Tor on and off, globally and per site; a site without Tor
  goes through whatever the system does (the VPN, in its current
  mode); with Tor on, every site gets its own Tor circuit (SOCKS
  username, IsolateSOCKSAuth) and its own fingerprint profile, tied
  together: one IP, one profile per site;
- answer every fingerprint probe with silence or a lie;
- wipe at quit every site that is not kept, with its profile; kept
  sites keep their data and their profile across restarts;
- show a "Sites" page with all data, profile and exit IP per site;
- look up each site's exit IP by itself (check.torproject.org, through
  that site's own circuit), again after ten minutes;
- be HTTPS-only, with a per-site list of sites allowed to fall back
  to plain HTTP when HTTPS fails;
  .onion and local addresses are exempt;
- detect other add-ons holding the "proxy" permission, which can send
  traffic around Tor, and warn about them.

Cookie banners are NOT handled by switch: that is left to the add-on
"I don't care about cookies", which install.sh installs.


2. The rules the work follows
-----------------------------

- Plain files, small and self-contained, no build system, no
  dependencies beyond IceCat itself (and curl and sed for install.sh).
- ASCII only, English only, lines at most 80 columns, 4-space indents,
  C-like style even in JavaScript: declarations at the top of each
  function, one side effect per statement, braces as in K&R with
  function braces on their own lines.
- XHTML 1.0 Transitional pages, CSS 1 plus a little CSS 2.
- No telemetry, no update checks, no unique IDs; nothing identifying
  (names, hosts, paths of a real user) in code or examples.
- Our own data in flat formats only ("host secret" lines, one host
  per line), never nested JSON of our own design.
- Scripts never use sudo; they run as the user, or say clearly that
  root is needed.


3. Files
--------

extension/           the extension (manifest v2, version 0.24)
  manifest.json      permissions include proxy, webRequest(Blocking),
                     management, browsingData, cookies, contentScripts
  background.js      everything: settings, Tor routing (proxy.onRequest),
                     isolation, HTTPS upgrade, header lies, wipes, IP
                     lookups, rival detection, messages from the pages
  face.js            builds a site's profile from a seed (shared by the
                     background and the page script)
  inject.js          page script: the lies told inside each page
  popup.html/.js     the toolbar popup
  options.html/.js   settings, keys 1-6 jump to the sections
  sites.html/.js     the per-site data, profile and IP page
  nohttps.html/.js   shown when a site has no HTTPS: h once, a always,
                     b or Esc back
  style.css          shared dark style
  icon.svg
install.sh           configures an installed IceCat profile (and makes
                     one when IceCat was never started):
                     ./install.sh [-n] [-u] [-x] [-f] [-d] [-w] [profile]
                     -n dry run, -u uninstall, -x skip add-ons,
                     -f replace add-ons, -d fetch without Tor,
                     -w only print the profile directory
                     env: TOR_SOCKS (127.0.0.1:9050), ICECAT_ROOT
icecat.prefs         166 prefs written to user.js; one per line,
                     "name value"; install.sh -u removes exactly these,
                     from user.js and prefs.js
addons.txt           GNUzilla catalogue numbers of the add-ons to fetch
                     (uBlock Origin, Dark Reader, I don't care about
                     cookies)
userChrome.css       habamax colours for IceCat's own window; replaced
                     only if absent or carrying the "switch: habamax"
                     marker
icecat               the dotfiles' icecat command (~/.system/bin/icecat):
                     the real IceCat, then tidy.sh -q once it quits
tidy.sh              removes the empty storage/default/<origin>
                     directories IceCat leaves behind, whose names leak
                     the sites visited: ./tidy.sh [-n] [-r] [profile]


4. Key design facts
-------------------

- Profiles: the seed is face_seed(secret, gen, site_gen, site). Kept
  hosts have their own lasting secret (state.faces, "host secret"
  lines); all other sites use the session secret, renewed at quit.
  New identity renews everything, kept hosts included.
- runtime.onStartup does not fire on the first start after an update,
  so the wipe of unkept sites runs whenever background.js loads (also
  when switch is turned off and on again in about:addons).
- Another extension's proxy.onRequest can override ours; that is why
  management is used to find and name such rivals.
- Tor is 127.0.0.1:9150, the port the dotfiles' torrc keeps for
  IceCat (../net/README); its default IsolateSOCKSAuth gives each SOCKS
  username its own circuit.
- icecat.prefs sets that same Tor proxy as IceCat's own, so whenever
  switch is not running (disabled, not started yet, a private window
  it may not enter) IceCat still goes through Tor; WebRTC is off there
  too. While switch runs, its own setting wins, so "Tor off" in switch
  is direct.
- install.sh sets network.proxy.failover_direct and allow_bypass to
  false, turns off HTTP/3 and DNS-over-HTTPS (trr.mode 5), so nothing
  escapes Tor; and keeps IceCat's own HTTPS-only mode off, because
  switch does it.
- A profile never makes up the OS: workers, fonts, scrollbars and the
  TCP stack would tell the real one. With resistFingerprinting on, the
  screen and cores are its own too, as CSS and workers report them; a
  profile is then its circuit plus its own canvas and audio noise.
- Frames from other sites take the profile of the site in the address
  bar (location.ancestorOrigins, else the referrer), as the circuit
  and the headers do.
- Kept sites: a cookie in a split jar (first-party isolation or a
  partition) is kept only when that jar's site is kept; otherwise a
  cookie is kept when its domain is a kept host, under one, or a parent
  of one (".example.org" for a kept www.example.org). Storage is wiped
  by host and its subdomains, so hosts bound up with a kept site are
  left out of it.
- Only switch's own pages may send it orders; messages from content
  scripts or anything else are refused.
- install.sh refuses to run while that profile is in use (it reads the
  profile's "lock" link and checks the PID in it).
- GNUzilla's pages link fixed xpi files on addons.mozilla.org, from
  2023; install.sh fetches the page and the xpi through Tor, checks
  the add-on id, and copies it to extensions/<id>.xpi.


5. State
--------

Version 0.24:

- The popup's switches sit in separate boxes: Tor, JavaScript,
  cookies, HTTP and keep, so the two JavaScript lines no longer blend
  into the others.

Version 0.23:

- The popup has "Keep <site>" (key p), and says what a kept site
  keeps.

Version 0.22:

- A site allowed HTTP is no longer always loaded over HTTP: it is
  tried over HTTPS first, and falls back to plain HTTP, without the
  question page, only when HTTPS fails (until IceCat quits).
- The buttons and labels say "Allow HTTP" (popup key h, the no-HTTPS
  page's h and a).

Version 0.21:

- Kept sites choose what they keep: cookies, storage, history,
  settings (JavaScript, Tor, cookies allowed, HTTP allowed, exempt) and
  profile. All of them by default; the lists nokeep_cookies,
  nokeep_storage, nokeep_history, nokeep_settings and nokeep_face (in
  Settings) name the kept sites that leave a part. On the Sites page,
  keys 1 to 5 switch them for the marked or selected sites, and the
  kept column shows the letters c s h p f.
- History is no longer cleared by IceCat at quit (that took kept
  sites' history too). The wipe at start removes the history of every
  site that does not keep it; so does forgetting a closed site. With
  no site kept, all history goes, as before.

Version 0.20 (the cause of sites surviving a quit):

- Settings saved by an older version had the wipe at start and the
  forgetting of closed sites both off, so nothing was ever wiped.
  Both are switched on once for settings from before 0.20; turning
  them off later in Settings is kept.
- The Sites page says so when either is off.
- The wipe at start clears the site lists first, before the slower
  cookie and storage work, so a wipe cut short still empties the list.

Version 0.19 (the wipe at start, made blunter while a report of data
surviving a quit is looked into):

- With no site kept, the wipe is one call to IceCat's own clearing of
  cookies, storage, cache, history, downloads and forms, which cannot
  miss a host.
- With kept sites, cookies of unkept hosts are also removed by IceCat
  by host name, besides one by one; history deletions are waited for.

Version 0.18:

- Cookies are switched like JavaScript: none by default, by header or
  by script (document.cookie, cookieStore), unless allowed for all
  (Settings) or for a site: popup key k, Sites page K for the marked
  sites, the list "Sites allowed cookies". A kept site needs its
  cookies allowed to stay logged in. The list empties of unkept sites
  at quit, like the others.
- The mark box on the Sites page is bigger.

Version 0.16:

- Sites page: every action on a site works on the marked sites (x,
  Space, *, u), or else on the selected one: p keep, f forget, c new
  face, i look up IP, h clear history, and new J (allow JavaScript) and
  T (without Tor), each turning all on, or all off when all are on.
- The pages' own keys no longer start IceCat's find bar
  (accessibility.typeaheadfind off; Ctrl+F and / still find).
- The bookmarks toolbar shows on every page.
- 0.17: the Sites page opens with the filter (search) focused; Enter
  or Esc goes to the list.

Version 0.15:

- Forgetting a site works: before, a site's cookies on its parent
  domain (".example.org" for www.example.org, often set by scripts,
  so sites with JavaScript on) stayed, a page still open wrote its
  data straight back, and a site in a list (JavaScript, HTTP, no Tor,
  exempt) stayed on the Sites page. Now forget closes the site's tabs
  first, then removes its data, its history, downloads, closed tabs and
  every list entry naming it, kept or not; cookies another kept site
  needs stay.
- Sites page: x or Space marks a site, * marks all shown, u unmarks
  all; f (forget) and p (keep) act on the marked sites, or else on the
  selected one.
- At quit, unkept sites also leave the lists (JavaScript allowed, HTTP
  allowed, no Tor, exempt): keep a site to keep its settings.
- At start, no request goes out until the wipe of unkept sites is done
  (at most ten seconds).
- IceCat itself now clears at every quit history (kept sites' too;
  bookmarks stay) and site settings (permissions, zoom, HSTS), besides
  cache, downloads, forms and the session.
- The icecat command runs tidy.sh after IceCat quits; tidy.sh now also
  removes AlternateServices, the session files, favicons.sqlite, and
  the thumbnails and cache directories, and checks storage/temporary.
- What can still be left on disk after a quit: cookies and storage of
  unkept sites whose tabs were open when IceCat quit, if IceCat closed
  before switch finished the wipe (an extension cannot hold up the
  quit). They are wiped at the next start, before any request.

Version 0.14:

- Tor per site, like JavaScript: the popup's "Tor on <site>" (key v)
  and the list "Sites that go without Tor" in Settings. A whole page
  goes one way, by the site in the address bar; switching a site wipes
  its data when "Wipe data and circuits when Tor is switched" is on.
- The defaults read pages rather than run them: JavaScript off, Tor
  on, web fonts blocked, a site forgotten when its last tab closes, no
  automatic exit IP lookups (key l still asks).
- icecat.prefs: pages get only IceCat's default fonts (no web fonts, no
  installed font asked for by name, only the fonts every system has),
  no preconnects, early hints or favicon fetches, Referer only within a
  site, no GPC or DNT header, no OCSP (CRLite checks certificates
  locally), no region lookup.


Version 0.13: the review of 0.12, fixed.

- Tor port 9150 (IceCat's), not 9050 (proxychains4's).
- The prefs hold Tor and keep WebRTC off when switch is not running.
- A profile's OS is the real one; screen and cores follow RFP when it
  is on; a lied screen is never smaller than the window.
- Frames from other sites used their own host as the profile seed, so
  one tracker frame looked the same on every site, and its JavaScript
  disagreed with its own headers.
- Keep: cookies of ".example.org" were wiped for a kept
  www.example.org (logged out of kept sites), and the storage wipe of
  the parent domain took the kept site's storage with it; a kept site's
  cookies from inside unkept sites survived.
- Cookies are wiped in every store (private windows, containers), and
  embedded sites' storage is wiped as well.
- ws:// is upgraded to wss:// like http://; the "keeps sending you back
  to HTTP" page opens again (a redirect to it needed it to be open to
  every site); "this time" ends at a wipe.
- No exit IP lookups for onions and local addresses.
- Content scripts are swapped without a gap; until they are first
  registered, pages get no JavaScript (the popup says so).
- If the proxy listener ever fails, the answer is still Tor.
- install.sh: makes the profile when there is none; -u also cleans
  prefs.js; a pref is matched by its exact name; manifest.json is
  read at most 1 MB from a downloaded xpi.
- tidy.sh checks the profile's lock instead of pgrep, and removes the
  HSTS list (SiteSecurityServiceState).

Not yet done or not yet checked:

- Nothing has been run in a real IceCat yet: the "no HTTPS" page,
  ancestorOrigins in frames, and the claimed profile at first start.
- "Run in Private Windows" has to be allowed by hand in about:addons.
- OffscreenCanvas and canvases in workers are not noised (RFP's own
  canvas randomisation covers them when it is on).


6. Ideas not yet taken up
-------------------------

- "I still don't care about cookies" (GNUzilla 2766861), the fork free
  of Avast, in place of the original; commented out in addons.txt.
- Tridactyl, for vim keys throughout IceCat.
- Bind "tidy.sh -r" to a key in dwm, so IceCat is always tidied after
  it quits.
